# MongoDB Rules

## lib/mongoose.js — only connection file

```js
import mongoose from "mongoose";
let cached = global.mongoose ?? { conn: null, promise: null };
global.mongoose = cached;

export async function connectDB() {
  if (cached.conn) return cached.conn;
  if (!cached.promise) {
    cached.promise = mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO_URI, { bufferCommands: false });
  }
  cached.conn = await cached.promise;
  return cached.conn;
}
```

- Never call `mongoose.connect()` anywhere else
- `bufferCommands: false` required

## Model pattern

```js
const schema = new mongoose.Schema({ ... });
export default mongoose.models.ModelName || mongoose.model("ModelName", schema);
```

## Query patterns

```js
// Read-only: always .lean()
const items = await Model.find({ userId }).lean();

// Exclude password
const user = await User.findById(id).select("-contrasena").lean();

// Check null
const item = await Model.findById(id).lean();
if (!item) return res.status(404).json({ mensaje: "No encontrado." });
```

## Atlas M0 limits

- No transactions
- No large `$lookup` aggregations
- Max 100 connections — cached pattern handles this
